    \title Debug Qt for Python applications

    You can debug Python applications that contain both Python and QML code with
    \l{debugpy} and the QML debugger.

    \section1 Debug Python code

    To debug only the Qt for Python code in an application, follow the
    instructions in \l{VS Code: Python debugging in VS Code}.

    To launch and debug Qt for Python applications:

    \list 1
        \li Open a \e {launch.json} file. For more information, see
            \l {Debug Qt applications}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Python Debugger}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Add Configuration}, and then select the
            \uicontrol {Qt: PySide: Launch} debug configuration.
    \endlist

    The configuration looks as follows:

    \badcode
    "configurations": [
        Pyside
        {
            "name": "Python Debugger: Current File",
            "type": "debugpy",
            "request": "launch",
            "program": "${file}",
            "console": "integratedTerminal"
        },
        {
            "name": "Qt: PySide: Launch",
            "type": "debugpy",
            "request": "launch",
            "program": "${workspaceFolder}/main.py",
            "preLaunchTask": "PySide: build"
        }
    ]
    \endcode

    \note If \c main.py is not the application entry point, change it in
    \c "program".

    \section1 Debug mixed Python and QML code

    You can create a debug configuration that first starts an application and
    then attaches both \c debugpy and the QML debugger to it using a port number
    that the \uicontrol {Qt: Acquire Port} pre-launch task acquires.

    \section2 Turn on QML debugging

    To turn on QML debugging, add the following code to your QML application:

    \badcode
    from pathlib import Path
    from argparse import ArgumentParser

    from PySide6.QtGui import QGuiApplication
    from PySide6.QtQml import QQmlApplicationEngine, QQmlDebuggingEnabler

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        argument_parser = ArgumentParser()
        argument_parser.add_argument("-qmljsdebugger", action="store",
                                     help="Enable QML debugging")
        options = argument_parser.parse_args()
        if options.qmljsdebugger:
            QQmlDebuggingEnabler.enableDebugging(True)

        app = QApplication(sys.argv)
        engine = QQmlApplicationEngine()
    \endcode

    \section2 Launch and debug applications

    The QML debugger connects to a socket server that is run by the QmlEngine
    instance. Specify the port number in the launch configuration.

    To debug a Qt Quick application that contains both Python and QML code:

    \list 1
        \li Open a \e {launch.json} file. For more information, see
            \l {Debug Qt applications}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Python Debugger}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Add Configuration}, and then select the
            \uicontrol {Qt: PySide: Launch with QML Debugger} debug
            configuration.
        \li In \c args, set the value of \c "port" to the port number to use:
            \badcode
            "configurations": [
                {
                "name": "Qt: PySide: Launch with QML debugger",
                "type": "debugpy",
                "request": "launch",
                "program": "${workspaceFolder}/main.py",
                "preLaunchTask": "PySide: build",
                "args": [
                    "-qmljsdebugger=port:<port_number>,block,services:DebugMessages,QmlDebugger,V8Debugger"
            ]
            \endcode
        \li Select \uicontrol {Add Configuration}, and then select the
            \uicontrol {Qt: QML: Attach by port} debug configuration.
        \li Set the port number as the value of the \c port option in the
            \e {launch.json} file:
            \badcode
            "configurations": [
                {
                    "name": "Qt: QML: Attach by port",
                    "type": "qml",
                    "request": "attach",
                    "host": "localhost",
                    "port": "<port_number>"
                },
            \endcode
        \li Add a compound launch task for Python/QML debugging to the
            \e {launch.json} file:
            \badcode
            "compounds": [
                {
                    "name": "Python/QML",
                    "configurations": [
                        "Qt: PySide: Launch with QML debugger,
                        "Qt: QML: Attach by port"
                    ]
                }
            ]
            \endcode
        \li Run the compound debug configuration.
    \endlist

    \section2 Automatically assign port numbers

    Use the \c ${command:qt-qml.debugPort} variable and a compound launch and
    pre-launch task to automatically assign port numbers:

    \badcode
    "configurations": [
        {
            "name": "Qt: PySide: Launch with QML debugger",
            "type": "debugpy",
            "request": "launch",
            "program": "${workspaceFolder}/main.py",
            "preLaunchTask": "PySide: build",
            "args": [
                "-qmljsdebugger=port:${command:qt-qml.debugPort},block,services:DebugMessages,QmlDebugger,V8Debugger"
                ]
        },
        {
            "name": "Qt: QML: Attach by port",
            "type": "qml",
            "request": "attach",
            "host": "localhost",
            "port": "${command:qt-qml.debugPort}"
        },
    ],
    "compounds": [
        {
            "name": "Python/QML",
            "configurations": [
                "Qt: PySide: Launch with QML debugger,
                "Qt: QML: Attach by port"
            ],
            "preLaunchTask": "Qt: Acquire Port",
        }
    ]
    \endcode


    \sa {Debug Qt applications}, {Debug Qt Quick applications},
    {VS Code: Debugging}, {VS Code:Python debugging in VS Code}
*/
